1
00:00:01,650 --> 00:00:05,396
When a heart stops, every second without compressions counts.

2
00:00:05,446 --> 00:00:06,562
This is Elvrek.

3
00:00:06,612 --> 00:00:12,240
A real-time safety controller for mechanical CPR, built on QNX 8.0.

4
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:20,942
Today a crew runs a CPR machine and a separate defibrillator, each on its own clock, and every handover between them is a pause.

5
00:00:20,992 --> 00:00:27,108
Elvrek puts compressions, breaths and the shock on one clock, ticking a thousand times a second.

6
00:00:27,158 --> 00:00:29,510
The AI only makes suggestions.

7
00:00:30,317 --> 00:00:33,774
On QNX, every job is its own small process.

8
00:00:33,824 --> 00:00:37,548
The safety process runs at the top priority on a timer pulse.

9
00:00:37,598 --> 00:00:43,617
The rhythm classifier runs unprivileged, and QNX refuses it the piston and the shock line.

10
00:00:44,117 --> 00:00:45,110
Now the real thing.

11
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:50,639
QNX 8.0 boots in QEMU, with the whole stack built with qcc.

12
00:00:50,689 --> 00:00:56,527
The piston and chest are a physics model, behind the interface a real motor driver will use.

13
00:00:57,417 --> 00:00:59,504
First, the classifier attacks.

14
00:00:59,554 --> 00:01:03,735
It tries the piston, the shock line and every other forbidden path.

15
00:01:03,785 --> 00:01:05,987
Seven of seven are refused.

16
00:01:06,883 --> 00:01:08,526
Next, a shockable rhythm.

17
00:01:08,576 --> 00:01:14,452
The charger fills while the piston keeps working, and the controller reads the rhythm through its own compressions.

18
00:01:14,502 --> 00:01:16,988
It is sure, so there is no pause to look.

19
00:01:17,038 --> 00:01:23,973
The shock fires, compressions are back within milliseconds, then carbon dioxide rises and the piston parks.

20
00:01:24,783 --> 00:01:28,950
This is the crew's screen, drawn with QNX Screen on the target.

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,104
It only reads.

22
00:01:30,154 --> 00:01:32,808
The crew sees charging, then shock delivered.

23
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:34,913
The pause was too short to show.

24
00:01:36,117 --> 00:01:39,166
Now we kill the safety process in the middle of a stroke.

25
00:01:39,216 --> 00:01:43,947
The driver's own watchdog lifts the cup, and nothing restarts the controller.

26
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:47,311
Elvrek also starts from the boot image itself.

27
00:01:47,361 --> 00:01:53,940
The first compression is done 1.4 seconds after the kernel starts, before the disks or the network are up.

28
00:01:55,350 --> 00:02:00,599
We ran the same arrest the usual way too, a CPR device plus a separate AED.

29
00:02:00,649 --> 00:02:05,707
The pause before the shock was twelve seconds, and five even when the crew pre-charges.

30
00:02:05,757 --> 00:02:09,730
With Elvrek it was three tenths of a second, the crew's own press.

31
00:02:11,117 --> 00:02:12,531
Every claim is checked.

32
00:02:12,581 --> 00:02:15,572
Twelve of twelve fault cases pass on QNX.

33
00:02:15,622 --> 00:02:19,875
A kernel trace finds no tick whose work ran past one millisecond.

34
00:02:19,925 --> 00:02:24,877
And thirty one safety requirements are traced to code and tests, automatically.

35
00:02:25,983 --> 00:02:29,573
Elvrek is open source, and it is not a medical device.

36
00:02:29,623 --> 00:02:33,203
Next stop, a Raspberry Pi 5 and a training manikin.

37
00:02:33,253 --> 00:02:35,083
Save lives, smarter.
